## What happened

.NET 11 throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` from `DSA.Create()` on macOS. Apple removed DSA from its Security framework years ago, and .NET previously worked around this by using OpenSSL (installed via Homebrew). In .NET 11, Microsoft completed the deprecation of DSA across the board — on macOS, the managed wrapper no longer falls back to OpenSSL automatically.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Use OpenSSL explicitly (recommended if you must keep verifying DSA signatures):**

```csharp
// Add package: System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl (if not using the shim)
// Ensure OpenSSL is available on the macOS host (e.g., via Homebrew).

using System.Security.Cryptography;

// Load the key and verify using the OpenSSL-backed implementation:
DSAOpenSsl dsaOpenSsl = new DSAOpenSsl();
dsaOpenSsl.ImportParameters(legacyParams);
bool valid = dsaOpenSsl.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA1);
```

**Option B — Migrate away from DSA entirely (best long-term):**

DSA (FIPS 186-2) is considered legacy. If you control both signer and verifier, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (supported natively on all .NET 11 platforms):

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
```

**Option C — Conditionalize at runtime:**

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
else
{
    // Use DSAOpenSsl or reject gracefully
}
```

## Summary

| | Linux | Windows | macOS |
|---|---|---|---|
| .NET 10 | ✅ (OpenSSL) | ✅ (CNG) | ✅ (OpenSSL fallback) |
| .NET 11 | ✅ (OpenSSL) | ✅ (CNG) | ❌ `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

The safest fix is to reference `DSAOpenSsl` directly on macOS (ensuring OpenSSL is installed), or — better yet — plan a migration to ECDSA/Ed25519.
